#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573275 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $2\\mathbf{b}+(-3\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}).$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$2\\mathbf{b}+(-3\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c})=\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\mathbf{b}+(-3\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}) & =2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+(-3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+(\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573276 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{s}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $3\\mathbf{r}+2\\mathbf{s}$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction., we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{3r+2s}{|3r+2s|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3r+2s & =3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-6)+0 \\\\ 3+(-6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3r+2s| & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $3r+2s$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{3r+2s}{|3r+2s|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}(3r+2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573283 :question/problem "![](images/45/453e8fc1c4d8a5262fd0a8aa7341dd71b909a4a72cec94cd2698f800f7dc06c9.png)\n\nLet $A(0,3)$ and $B(4,-1).$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=0i+3j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=4i-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i-j)-(0i+3j) \\\\  & =4i-4j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573284 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{c}=⟨12,-18⟩$, then $-\\frac{1}{3}\\overset{\\to }{c}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $-\\frac{1}{3}\\overset{\\to }{c},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}\\overset{\\to }{c} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ⟨12,-18⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot 12,\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot (-18)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573287 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{u}=⟨2,3⟩$, then what is the magnitude of $\\mathbf{u}?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,u\\,| & =\\sqrt{{u}_{x}^{2}+{u}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573288 :question/problem "![](images/b9/b911126b4468e016afbab7faaf20223d0811827bb7f57cdefc2f1923cb7faf1b.png)\n\nPoints $A$ and $C$ are shown in the picture above. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{AC}|.$" :question/worked-solution "From the picture, we obtain $A(0,3)$ and $C(4,0).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=0i+3j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=4i+0j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\overset{\\to }{OC}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i)-(3j) \\\\  & =4i-3j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AC}| & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573293 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{p}=⟨-1,5⟩,$ then $3\\overset{\\to }{p}=$$\\,⟨$\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\overset{\\to }{p} & =3\\cdot ⟨-1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\cdot (-1),\\,3\\cdot 5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,15⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573294 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨7,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨9,11⟩.$ Find $-2\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $-2\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} & =-2\\cdot ⟨7,4⟩+⟨9,11⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-14,-8⟩+⟨9,11⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573295 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{u}=⟨-3,-5⟩$, then what is the magnitude of $\\mathbf{u}?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,u\\,| & =\\sqrt{{u}_{x}^{2}+{u}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573297 :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf7890789d1b80002ac2055fd61e8c8f58525f9bb1714bd77ff6e8542567e962.png)\n\nLet $A(-2,0)$ and $B(3,3).$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=-2i+0j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=3i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+3j)-(-2i+0j) \\\\  & =5i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573298 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{a}=5\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j},$ then $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}=${{field-1}}$\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}+$\n{{field-2}}$\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b},$ we just need to compute all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & =(5\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j})+(-\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =5\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{i})+(-3\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573300 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-2,7⟩.$ Find $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+49} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{53}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573310 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{u}=⟨4,-3⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{v}=⟨-1,2⟩$, then $2\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v} & =2\\cdot ⟨4,-3⟩+⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-6⟩+⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8+(-1),-6+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8-1,-6+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573311 :question/problem "Let $\\overset{\\to }{U}=⟨7,-1⟩.$ Find $|\\,\\overset{\\to }{U}|.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,\\overset{\\to }{U}| & =\\sqrt{{u}_{x}^{2}+{u}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(7)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573312 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\hspace{0.167em}4\\mathbf{a}-(3\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{c}).$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$4\\mathbf{a}-(3\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{c})=\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\mathbf{a}-(3\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{c}) & =4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-(3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}-2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-28 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573315 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{m}=⟨-6,2⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{n}=⟨5,-8⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{m}-\\overset{\\to }{n}=$$\\,⟨$\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{m}-\\overset{\\to }{n} & =⟨-6,2⟩-⟨5,-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6-5,\\,2-(-8)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-11,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573319 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-4,6⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨3,-5⟩$, then $\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}=$$\\,⟨$\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $a-b,$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-b & =⟨-4,6⟩-⟨3,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4-3,6-(-5)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573320 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{r}$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{r}{|\\,r\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,r\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $r$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{r}{|r|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot r \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573869 :question/problem "Find $∇f(x,y,z),$  given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{1/2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{z}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}\\right)i+\\left(-\\frac{y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}\\right)j+\\left(-\\frac{z}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}\\right)k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}(x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573871 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}{e}^{x/y},$ find $∇f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x}{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}){e}^{x/y} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{x/y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x}{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}){e}^{x/y} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-x}{xy}{e}^{x/y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{x/y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y-x}{xy}{e}^{x/y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573893 :question/problem "Evaluate the gradient of $g(x,y)$ at the point $({e}^{2},1)$ if $g(x,y)=(x+y)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})+(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}+1+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})+(x+y)\\cdot (-2y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}-2xy-2{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-3{y}^{2}-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),\\,{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}+1+\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-3{y}^{2}-2xy⟩ \\\\ ∇g({e}^{2},1) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})-{1}^{2}+1+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})-3\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot {e}^{2}\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2+{e}^{-2},-1-2{e}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573901 :question/problem "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z)$ if $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ and $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=f∇h+h∇f.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)=yz \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)=xz \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz)=xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\text{ }k=yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $h(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))=\\frac{1}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z)] & =f(x,y,z)∇h(x,y,z)+h(x,y,z)∇f(x,y,z) \\\\  & =xyz(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)(yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =\\frac{xyz}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{xyz}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{xyz}{z}\\text{ }k+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)(yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)(yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))(yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573903 :question/problem "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$ and $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=f\\,∇\\,g+g\\,∇\\,f\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the first order partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y})={e}^{x+y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y})={e}^{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j={e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\text{ }j=2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =({e}^{x+y})(2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j)+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})({e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+2y{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x+{y}^{2}){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}+2y+{y}^{2}){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573928 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)h(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right),\\hspace{2em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y) & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\hspace{2em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}h(x,y)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=h∇f+f∇h.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇h(x,y) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j)+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j+\\frac{x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{y+x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)-x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}((y+x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right))i+(y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)-x)j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573938 :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y)=({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}){e}^{x}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}){e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{x}+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}){e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}){e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-2y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y)=({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}){e}^{x}\\,i-2y{e}^{x}\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇h(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}){e}^{x}\\,i-2y{e}^{x}\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}){e}^{x} &=0, \\\\ -2y{e}^{x} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+2x-{0}^{2}){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x(x+2){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y)=0$ at two points: $(-2,0)$ and $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573946 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =x+y,\\hspace{1em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\operatorname{ln}(x+y),\\hspace{1em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x+y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{x+y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,g\n$$\n\nSo, using this product rule we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)(\\text{ }i+\\text{ }j)+(x+y)(\\frac{1}{x+y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{x+y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\text{ }j+\\frac{x+y}{x+y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{x+y}{x+y}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\text{ }i+\\text{ }i)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\text{ }j+1\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575533 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,-1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y}-y)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{x+y}-x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(\\frac{1}{2+(-1)}-(-1))\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{2+(-1)}-2)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,-1)}{‖∇f(2,-1)‖} & =\\frac{2i-j}{\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2i-j}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575546 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xy+2x+y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xy+2x+y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xy+2x+y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2x+y+2)\\text{ }i+(-2y+x+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =(2(1)+(-1)+2)\\text{ }i+(-2(-1)+1+1)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,-1)}{‖∇f(1,-1)‖} & =\\frac{3\\,i+4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575562 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy+8y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,-2)$, can be found by computing $∇f(2,-2).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy+8y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy+8y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2x-2y)\\text{ }i+(2y-2x+8)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(2,-2) & =(2(2)-2(-2))\\text{ }i+(2(-2)-2(2)+8)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =8\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,-2)}{‖∇f(2,-2)‖} & =\\frac{8\\,i}{\\sqrt{{8}^{2}}}=i\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575592 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =\\frac{2(1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(-1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,-1)}{‖∇f(1,-1)‖} & =\\frac{i-j}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{i-j}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575627 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{e}^{xyz}+{y}^{2},$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{xyz}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{xyz}+{y}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{e}^{xyz}+{y}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(2x+{x}^{2}yz){e}^{xyz}\\,i+({x}^{3}z{e}^{xyz}+2y)\\,j+{x}^{3}y{e}^{xyz}\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,0) & =(2(1)+({1}^{2})(-2)(0)){e}^{1(-2)(0)}\\,i+(({1}^{3})(0){e}^{1(-2)(0)}+2(-2))\\,j+({1}^{3})(-2){e}^{1(-2)(0)}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,j-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\,i-4\\,j-2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578794 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}-{e}^{-t},\\,y=t{e}^{t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}+{e}^{-t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=(t+1){e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(t+1){e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}+{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+t}{1+{e}^{-2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=0,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1+0}{1+{e}^{-0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578802 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0bd043f0ff9b6cc575d7912776bd0aaa41dc161b32304115cf6c8dedba86f81a.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578804 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=-\\frac{3}{t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\,\\frac{3}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{3}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot 0+1} \\\\  & =-\\,3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578806 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=x\\cdot f(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0.$\n\nSlope $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. \n\nIn part 2 we found that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}+f\\frac{\\text{d}(x)}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(2-3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(3+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =(2-3(0))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+(3+2(0))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578807 :question/problem "![](images/3a/3af9e0f9483bf968ebf0db637e74c0536f19d4da6f15895d03ab04f39d1d98b0.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$\n\n![](images/f9/f95b72a0b508e0cdacef7de386888f3b473aeba01574ce5fb345b9fec74bb900.png)\n\nIn $△AHB$, we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=2$ and $BH=2.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b| & =AB \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $a+b$ is $2\\sqrt{2}.$ Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{1}{|a+b|}\\cdot (a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578844 :question/problem "![](images/b1/b165889c6169a417f4d56f544aec8a049685693ff2b01ea13d51a9f4d841be4c.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-t,\\,y={t}^{2}-4,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-1,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t}{3{t}^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=3$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3}{3({3}^{2})-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{27-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578854 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude \n $20$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude  $30.$ Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbf{+}\\mathbf{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$  \n\n![](images/31/3164bddbade652f3268832c5c3551f5e30263ceca95a3696d0da1ef5362853cb.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b,$ we have to divide by the magnitude: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2} \\\\  & ={20}^{2}+{30}^{2} \\\\  & =400+900 \\\\  & =1300\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{1300}=10\\sqrt{13},\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10\\sqrt{13}}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578859 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed horizontally to the left and has magnitude \n $8$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed vertically up and has magnitude $6.$ Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbf{+}\\mathbf{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$   \n\n![](images/19/19a22ffd49677b2ff17b4c69908280dcfcf30695c63ae4bb6e4ab83441146194.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b,$ we have to divide by the magnitude: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2} \\\\  & ={8}^{2}+{6}^{2} \\\\  & =64+36 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{100}=10\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578873 :question/problem "![](images/85/853358cc3207d3f2738ecc3bd2ee6391d62b1a51735c43e16f37a2b83414800e.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t+1),\\,y=t{e}^{t},\\,-\\frac{1}{3}<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{3}{3t+1},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=(t+1){e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(t+1){e}^{t}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{3t+1}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(t+1)(3t+1){e}^{t}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=0,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,0} & =\\frac{(0+1)(0+1){e}^{0}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
